49 
50 `bindReactiveClient` supports one live transport. An additional different live
51 binding throws. A closed transport cannot be bound. `notifySessionChanged` calls
52 `setAuth` once and forces the first getter request to bypass the host cache; later
53 requests are forwarded unchanged. It throws when unbound or closed and drops the
54 stored closed binding when notified. It creates no additional client or polling loop. Convex handles outdated
55 credential-fetch races itself; the adapter separately fences diagnostic updates and
56 one bounded deferred identity-removal event task.
57 
58 ## Reactive failures and Convex 1.46 transport workaround
59 
60 `HostAuthFailure` is a readonly `{ code, attemptId }` snapshot, where code is
61 `"HOST_TOKEN_FETCH_FAILED"` or `"INVALID_HOST_TOKEN"`. It contains no token,
62 original error/message/stack, host claims or identity. `HostAuthErrorHandler` is
63 `(failure: HostAuthFailure) => void | Promise<void>`. Optional
64 `CortexConfig.onAuthError` observes new current reactive failures. The handler is
65 validated before transport allocation, and synchronous throws/async rejections from
66 it are isolated without logging or blocking null/deauthentication.
67 
68 `credentials.authFailure` returns the latest frozen current reactive failure.
69 Only a later current successful JWT or null getter result clears it. Configuration
70 and callback attempt sequence fences prevent older delayed successes/rejections from
71 clearing or overwriting a newer diagnostic or recovered session. An auth-status true
72 notification does not clear a pending getter diagnostic. Closed transports ignore
73 late adapter completions and deferred delivery. Standalone token/header/HTTP errors
74 remain ordinary rejected promises; this state concerns the reactive adapter only.
75 
76 The installed Convex 1.46 BaseConvexClient invokes AuthenticationManager.setConfig
77 without awaiting/catching its promise. Passing the throwing direct getter into
78 setAuth causes an unhandled rejection and leaves the socket paused. The reactive
79 adapter catches host/shape/asynchrony failures, publishes only the fixed diagnostic,
80 and returns null so the official manager can resume unauthenticated. Official
81 initial null handling may request the host getter a second time with forced refresh;
82 each failed latest attempt is observable rather than a hidden operation replay.
83 
84 The installed manager has another transport gap: clearAuth during setConfig's pause
85 clears local identity, but its Authenticate(None) message is dropped and resume does
86 not send an absent identity. The adapter uses supported public APIs only: before
87 explicit configuration/session changes it calls `client.client.clearAuth()` when an
88 identity is attached. Its official setAuth status-false callback schedules one
89 `setTimeout(..., 0)` event task to clearAuth again after promise continuations resume
90 the socket, ensuring wire identity removal. That task is fenced by current config,
91 latest attempt and client.closed. Reconfiguration or another false notification
92 cancels the older pending task. It does not inspect private paused state, patch the
93 installed library, poll or redispatch an operation. Future Convex updates must
94 requalify this installed-version workaround.
95 
96 Real installed-client tests use an inert opened WebSocket and record actual protocol
97 messages in memory. They verify rejecting/malformed/synchronous getters, synchronous
98 and async observer failures, no unhandled rejection, resumed query delivery, explicit
99 Authenticate(None), recovery, null/logout/login, server-rejected-token refresh,
100 overlapping notifications while paused, stale completions and closed/deferred fences.
101 This is actual client/manager qualification, not managed signature/grant verification.
102 
